WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.762
This slide applies the ON-versus-WHERE rule to a runnable PostgreSQL example.

00:00:04.762 --> 00:00:07.487
The tiny input shows three patients.

00:00:07.487 --> 00:00:11.022
Ana has both a Glucose and a Cholesterol result.

00:00:11.022 --> 00:00:15.767
Ben has only Cholesterol. Cara has no lab result.

00:00:15.767 --> 00:00:20.302
Our business question is not show patients who have Glucose tests.

00:00:20.302 --> 00:00:26.037
The question is show every patient, and attach a Glucose result when one exists.

00:00:26.037 --> 00:00:29.837
That wording tells us that every patient must remain.

00:00:29.837 --> 00:00:36.042
The Glucose condition belongs inside ON because it defines which lab rows are allowed to match.

00:00:36.042 --> 00:00:38.492
Ana gets her Glucose result.

00:00:38.492 --> 00:00:44.642
Ben remains but gets NULL lab values because his Cholesterol row is not eligible to match.

00:00:44.642 --> 00:00:49.852
Cara also remains with NULL lab values because there is no lab row at all.

00:00:49.852 --> 00:00:54.522
Run the query and verify that the full patient population remains.

00:00:54.522 --> 00:00:57.542
This is a very useful pattern for reporting.

00:00:57.542 --> 00:01:01.297
You may want every product with sales from a specific month,

00:01:01.297 --> 00:01:04.852
every student with a tutoring visit from a specific semester,

00:01:04.852 --> 00:01:09.217
or every employee with a required certification when one exists.

00:01:09.217 --> 00:01:16.407
The mental sentence is: preserve every patient, and among lab records allow only Glucose rows to match.
